      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "**The Joyless Street** (1925) stands as one of the most powerful social dramas of the Weimar era, directed by G.W. Pabst in his breakthrough film. Greta Garbo delivers a devastating performance as a middle-class woman driven to near-prostitution by post-WWI inflation in Vienna. The film's unflinching look at economic desperation influenced later works like **Bicycle Thieves** (1948) and **Mamma Roma** (1962). Pabst's stark realism and attention to psychological detail established him as a master of the New Objectivity movement, alongside contemporaries like **The Last Laugh** (1924)."
      },
